**New evidence**

- [Respect for human rights must be central during the elections in Brazil](https://www.amnesty.org/en/latest/news/2022/09/respect-for-human-rights-must-be-central-during-the-elections-in-brazil/) argues that Bolsonaro had consistently used anti-human-rights discourse, intensified it in government, and questioned the legitimacy of the STF and TSE during the 2022 campaign.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Respect for human rights must be central during the elections in Brazil","publisher":"Amnesty International","publication_date":"2022-09-20","url":"https://www.amnesty.org/en/latest/news/2022/09/respect-for-human-rights-must-be-central-during-the-elections-in-brazil/","claim":"Amnesty said Bolsonaro had consistently used anti-human-rights discourse, intensified it in government, and questioned the legitimacy of the STF and TSE during the 2022 election campaign.","locator":"lines 64-76","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct contemporaneous NGO commentary focused on the 2022 election context.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 election period.","ruler_attribution":"Directly about Bolsonaro's campaign discourse and governing style.","contrary_evidence":["It is advocacy-oriented and does not quantify violence by itself."],"lenses":["3B.3","3B.6","3B.8","3B.9"]}

- [World Report 2023: Brazil](https://www.hrw.org/world-report/2023/country-chapters/brazil) says Bolsonaro harassed and insulted Supreme Court justices and journalists, undermined trust in the electoral system with unproven fraud claims, and presided over a campaign season in which political violence rose.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"World Report 2023: Brazil","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2023","url":"https://www.hrw.org/world-report/2023/country-chapters/brazil","claim":"HRW reported that Bolsonaro harassed and insulted Supreme Court justices and journalists, tried to undermine trust in the electoral system with unproven fraud claims, and that political violence rose during the campaign season.","locator":"lines 73-85","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Major rights-monitoring report summarizing 2022 events and their effects.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Events of 2022 summarized in a 2023 country chapter.","ruler_attribution":"Directly tied to Bolsonaro's campaign and presidential conduct.","contrary_evidence":["The report is broad and includes both Bolsonaro-era climate and later institutional responses."],"lenses":["3B.3","3B.6","3B.8","3B.9","3B.10"]}

- [World Report 2023: Brazil](https://www.hrw.org/world-report/2023/country-chapters/brazil) also says police killed 6,145 people nationwide in 2021, Rio state police killed 1,011 people from January through October 2022, and investigations were too weak to secure accountability for deadly raids.
